From 2d11585d968bd24847d32aa3089c0b875966fbab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lucas Santos <lsantos7654@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Apr 2026 22:38:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 22/27] Force Connection: close on SSE liveness route
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0009's per-session counter leaked when the TUI navigated between
sessions: the client called startSSE -> ctrl.abort() -> new fetch on
the same TCP connection (HTTP/1.1 keepalive). The underlying socket
stayed open for the next request, so:
  - c.req.raw.signal didn't fire (socket alive),
  - stream.onAbort didn't fire (same reason),
  - the heartbeat write either hadn't happened yet or succeeded
    silently into the half-closed response.

Result: _decr never ran for the previous sessionID. Each navigation
left a stale entry in _subscriberCount; with two TUIs navigating, the
map filled to 5+ entries vs. the expected 2.

Setting `Connection: close` on the response disables HTTP/1.1
keepalive specifically for this route. Each SSE subscription now gets
its own TCP socket; aborting the fetch closes the socket; the signal
fires; _decr runs. Per-subscription TCP overhead is negligible — SSE
streams are long-lived (one socket per attached session, held until
the user navigates away or quits the TUI).

Patch 0013's c.req.raw.signal listener is still the right detection
mechanism — this patch just ensures the signal actually fires in the
keepalive scenario.
---
 packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts b/packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts
index 1b1488d..fd9dfe5 100644
--- a/packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts
+++ b/packages/opencode/src/server/routes/event.ts
@@ -79,6 +79,17 @@ export const EventRoutes = () =>
       c.header("Cache-Control", "no-cache, no-transform")
       c.header("X-Accel-Buffering", "no")
       c.header("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff")
+      // Hivemind: disable HTTP/1.1 keepalive on the SSE liveness route. Without
+      // this, when the TUI's startSSE -> ctrl.abort() -> new fetch flow runs,
+      // the underlying TCP socket is preserved for the next request. Bun's
+      // `c.req.raw.signal` doesn't fire (socket still open), `stream.onAbort`
+      // doesn't fire either, the heartbeat write may succeed silently into the
+      // half-closed response, and the previous sessionID's `_decr` never runs.
+      // Setting `Connection: close` makes each SSE subscription get its own
+      // TCP socket — fetch abort closes the socket, the signal fires, and
+      // _decr runs. Per-subscription TCP overhead is negligible (SSE streams
+      // are long-lived; one socket per attached session).
+      c.header("Connection", "close")
       return streamSSE(c, async (stream) => {
         const q = new AsyncQueue<string | null>()
         let done = false
